#!RuleTest
#test CDISC-SEND-0070 expect=violation domain=LB
#note "THE DEAD-GATE PIN (Fix #287 / M3-D4). There is NO LBSTDTC and NO LBENDTC column -- the ordinary shape of a real SENDIG findings dataset. Until M3-D4, Requirements.Variables.All was the CONJUNCTION [--DTC, --STDTC, --ENDTC]; --DTC resolved to LBDTC and passed, --STDTC resolved to the absent LBSTDTC, and describeVariablesMismatch returned there, so the rule reported SKIPPED without evaluating anything. Measured over the CDISC Library variable metadata, ZERO SEND domains in ANY package carry all three -- findings domains have --DTC and never --STDTC, events and interventions the reverse -- so the rule had been dead since authoring in all six packages, and the only way to exercise it at all was to fabricate a column the standard does not define. With the block deleted the operations self-neutralise on an absent column (OperationExecutor returns null when getColumnIndex < 0, and EmptyResult.PREDICATE makes `$x == true` false), so the change can only ADD findings. Row 1's halves are tier 10 and tier 7 -> FIRES; row 2 is clean."
#library standard=sendig version=3-1-1
#expectViolationCount 1
#expectViolationAt row=1 LBSEQ=1
dataset LB class=FINDINGS label="Laboratory Test Results"
col STUDYID  type=Char label="Study Identifier"
col DOMAIN   type=Char label="Domain Abbreviation"
col USUBJID  type=Char label="Unique Subject Identifier"
col LBSEQ    type=Num  label="Sequence Number"
col LBTESTCD type=Char label="Test Short Name"
col LBDTC    type=Char label="Date/Time of Specimen Collection"
---
S1 | LB | 001 | 1 | ALB | 2023-01-15/2023-01
S1 | LB | 001 | 2 | ALB | 2023-01-15/2023-01-20
---
